AUDIO: '[music] &gt;&gt; All right, YouTube. What is good? It\'s your boy Rebel back with another video. And today we are going to be taking a look at a new compression format called w4a8.'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, last week KeyJAI was in the comfy kitchen GitHub repo discussing the differences between int8 and the new w4a8 format. Now, the strict difference'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'between the two formats really isn\'t much other than the fact that the w4a8 is actually just a quanted int8. So, this is like a new age form of guff'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'format in that you\'re not compressing the full model, you are compressing the int8 con rod weights. So, for context, a guff format model is a bf16 format'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'converted into f16 and then compressed down into your quant ladder, which is your q2 through q8. And this w4a8 model is an int8 compressed down into four-bit'

[01:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'weight storage. So, you\'re getting the full int8 con rod activations on decompression when it dequantizes into the full int8 model. The reason that we'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use guff format models in the first place is because it compresses the model down to a usable size on smaller vram requirements. And then what we do is we open back up each of those layers during'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'diffusion and recompress them back into that small format. So, you\'re never really pulling the full weight of the model, you\'re holding a compressed version of it. This is the same concept as this, only with int8. So, you\'re'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'getting, you know, the CUDA kernel speed ups. You\'re getting, you know, way less quality impact cost, way less, you know, time for generations. The This cuts your generation time, at least on image'

